INT. BOTANICAL GARDEN - GREENHOUSE - DAY

Humid glass. Orchids and palms. Gravel paths. Mist nozzles hiss.

SUKI (30s, tourist cardigan, a camera around her neck) pretends to photograph a fern. MOSES (50s, bucket hat, binoculars) pretends to read a plaque. A GARDENER (60s, canvas apron, pruners) clips a hedge nearby, whistling.

SUKI
He hasn't looked at us.

MOSES
He's looked at us three times.

SUKI
Then he's bored.

MOSES
He's a gardener. He's trained to be bored.

SUKI
Do you have the schedule?

MOSES
Guards change at two. They leave the east door unlocked for the delivery.

SUKI
And the orchid?

MOSES
Third bench. Behind the staghorn. Blue pot.

SUKI
How much?

MOSES
Four hundred thousand to the right collector.

SUKI
For a flower.

MOSES
For the last one.

The Gardener's whistle stops. He sets down his pruners.

GARDENER
It's not the last one.

Suki and Moses turn, slowly.

SUKI
Excuse me?

GARDENER
The blue Vanda coerulea. There are eleven in the cold house. You're welcome to look.

MOSES
We were just admiring...

GARDENER
Admire all you like. My name is Ulrich. I've been the keeper here for thirty-six years, and I know what you're after.

SUKI
(to Moses)
Told you.

MOSES
Don't.

ULRICH
I'll make you a deal. You leave the blue pot. I'll let you take a cutting. A real one. Ready to root.

SUKI
Why?

ULRICH
Because the right collector is a man named Prosser who kills his plants, and I'd rather see one live.

MOSES
(slowly)
Show us the cold house.

ULRICH
Follow me. And put the camera away. It frightens the ferns.
